      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine how $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are related, and then use this relationship to find the value of $\\alpha$ when $\\beta = -72$.\n\nFirst, let's recall the definition of inverse proportionality. If two variables $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are inversely proportional, then their product is constant. This can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k\n\\]\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nGiven that $\\alpha = 4$ when $\\beta = 9$, we can find $k$ by substituting these values into the equation:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = 36\n\\]\n\nNow we can use this constant $k$ to find $\\alpha$ when $\\beta = -72$. Substituting $\\beta = -72$ and $k = 36$ into the equation $\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k$, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\nTo solve for $\\alpha$, we can divide both sides of the equation by $-72$:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we can write the Python code to verify this solution.\n```python\n# Given values\nalpha_initial = 4\nbeta_initial = 9\n\n# Calculate the constant k\nk = alpha_initial * beta_initial\n\n# New value of beta\nbeta_new = -72\n\n# Calculate the new value of alpha\nalpha_new = k / beta_new\nprint(alpha_new)\n```\n```output\n-0.5\n```\nThe value of $\\alpha$ when $\\beta = -72$ is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\).",
